$$2x + 6y = 5$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '1', ARRAY['1'], E'A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are parallel but not coincident. Two lines are parallel when the ratios of their corresponding coefficients are equal: $\\frac{k}{2} = \\frac{3}{6}$. Simplifying the right side gives $\\frac{k}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}$, so $k = 1$. To confirm the lines are not coincident, check the constant ratio: $\\frac{10}{5} = 2$, which does not equal $\\frac{1}{2}$. Since the coefficient ratios are equal but the constant ratio differs, the lines are parallel and distinct, confirming no solution exists when $k = 1$.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 110, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);
















